  "message": "I fixed the hull damage on takeoff from a ground-station pad. The code compiles, but I couldn't run the game here, so I haven't checked in-game that the hull now stays at 100%.\n\n**What the recording shows:** a manual takeoff from a pad at a Mars ground station (cruise speed 2 m/s, going up). The hull readout drops from 100% to 99% as soon as the thrusters fire, before the ship visibly touches anything.\n\n**Cause:** when docked, the ship's landing point sits exactly on the pad's collision surface. In `Player::DoFixspeedTakeoff` (`src/Player.cpp`), the step that lifts the ship 1 cm off the pad before it starts flying only ran for orbital stations. At a ground station the ship became collidable while still touching the pad. The first physics step counted that as a collision with the station, and since the ship no longer belongs to a docking bay, the station treated it as an impact and damaged the hull. This happens on every pad of every ground station.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **`src/Player.cpp`:** the 1 cm lift and a small upward starting velocity (0.05 m/s) now apply to every takeoff. Matching the station's rotation (direction, spin and sideways speed) still only happens for orbital stations, as before.\n- **`src/SpaceStation.cpp` (`UNDOCK_END`):** autopilot takeoffs from ground stations had the same zero-gap problem, so they get the same 1 cm lift before the upward thrust starts.\n\nNothing else about ships or stations changes.\n\nThe fix assumes each ship's lowest collision point is no lower than its landing tag; I couldn't measure this from the models here. If a ship's hull reaches more than about 1 cm below its tag, it would still touch the pad on takeoff and take damage. A takeoff from a Mars ground station pad, watching the hull readout, would settle it.",
